One common way the Java API is misused is through the improper handling of user input when interacting with the operating system, such as when using `Runtime.getRuntime().exec()`. If input is not sanitized, it can lead to Command Injection.

```java
import java.io.*;

public class CommandExecutor {
    // This method is vulnerable to Command Injection because it 
    // concatenates raw user input directly into a shell command.
    public void executeSystemCommand(String fileName) throws IOException {
        String command = "ls -l " + fileName; // String concatenation

        // A malicious user could provide: "file.txt; rm -rf /"
        Runtime.getRuntime().exec(command);
    }
}
```

***

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.List;

public class CommandExecutor {
    // This method is secure because it uses a ProcessBuilder with a List of arguments.
    // This treats the input strictly as a single argument rather than part of a shell command.
    public void executeSystemCommand(String fileName) throws IOException {
        // Create a ProcessBuilder with the command and arguments separated
        ProcessBuilder pb = new ProcessBuilder("ls", "-l", fileName);

        // Even if fileName contains "; rm -rf /", it will be treated 
        // as a literal filename and not executed as a command.
        pb.start();
    }
}
```

